1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,982
Thanks to all of you for coming along today to hear about how the

2
00:00:03,982 --> 00:00:07,340
robotic float project is helping with ocean research.

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,912
Well, first of all we'll look at what a robotic float does

4
00:00:10,912 --> 00:00:11,840
and its use.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,455
So let's start with the device itself.

6
00:00:14,455 --> 00:00:17,334
It looks a bit like a cigar and it's about one

7
00:00:17,334 --> 00:00:18,860
and a half metres long.

8
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:23,200
More importantly it's full of equipment that's designed to collect data.

9
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,631
So, it can help us in building up a profile of different factors which

10
00:00:27,631 --> 00:00:29,530
work together within the world's oceans.

11
00:00:29,530 --> 00:00:34,600
Sounds like a big project - isn't it too big for one country to undertake?

12
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:40,200
That's quite true but this project is a really good example of international co-operation.

13
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:46,216
Over the last five years scientists from thirteen countries have been taking part in the project

14
00:00:46,216 --> 00:00:49,600
and launching floats in their area of ocean control.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,360
And next year this number will rise to fourteen when Indonesia joins the project.

16
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:55,780
That's impressive.

17
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:58,830
But let's move onto how floats work.

18
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:01,960
The operational cycle goes like this.

19
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:06,509
Each of the floats is dropped in the ocean from a boat at a set point

20
00:01:06,509 --> 00:01:07,930
and activated from a satellite.

21
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:11,488
Then the float immediately sinks about 2,000 metres...

22
00:01:11,488 --> 00:01:14,650
that's two whole kilometres down in the water.

23
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:18,902
It stays at this depth for about 10 days and is carried around by

24
00:01:18,902 --> 00:01:21,940
the currents which operate in the ocean at this level.

25
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:26,307
During this time it's possible for it to cover quite large distances

26
00:01:26,307 --> 00:01:28,490
but the average is fifty kilometres.

27
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:31,440
So what is it actually recording?

28
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,447
Well at this stage nothing,

29
00:01:33,447 --> 00:01:39,066
but as it rises to the surface it collects all sorts of data, most

30
00:01:39,066 --> 00:01:40,671
importantly variations in salinity,

31
00:01:40,671 --> 00:01:41,875
that's salt levels,

32
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:46,290
and the changes in temperature, a bit like underwater weather balloons.

33
00:01:46,290 --> 00:01:50,991
Then when it gets back to the surface all the data it's collected is

34
00:01:50,991 --> 00:01:52,670
beamed up to the satellite.

35
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:58,559
After about five hours on the surface the float automatically sinks, beginning the whole

36
00:01:58,559 --> 00:01:59,400
process again.

37
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,460
What happens to the data?

38
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:07,056
Well the information is transferred direct to onshore meteorological stations.., like our one in

39
00:02:07,056 --> 00:02:07,455
Hobart...

40
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:13,451
and within four hours the findings can be on computers and they can be mapped

41
00:02:13,451 --> 00:02:14,250
and analysed.

42
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:18,136
You say you're building models of the world's ocean systems

43
00:02:18,136 --> 00:02:20,856
but how're they going to be used,

44
00:02:20,856 --> 00:02:22,021
and more importantly,

45
00:02:22,021 --> 00:02:22,410
when?

46
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:26,400
Some of the data has already helped in completing projects.

47
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:32,000
For example, our understanding of the underlying causes of El Nino events is being

48
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,600
confirmed by float data.

49
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:39,218
Another way we're using float data is to help us to understand the mechanics

50
00:02:39,218 --> 00:02:42,830
of climate change, like global warming and ozone depletion.

51
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:47,039
That's part of an ongoing variability study but the results are still a long

52
00:02:47,039 --> 00:02:47,640
way off.

53
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:52,500
However, this is not the case with our ocean weather forecasting.

54
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,700
Because we know from the floats what the prevailing weather conditions will be in

55
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:00,300
certain parts of the ocean, we can advise the navy on search

56
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:01,200
and rescue missions.

57
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:05,899
That's happening right now and many yachtsmen owe their lives to the success of

58
00:03:05,899 --> 00:03:06,570
this project.

59
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:11,511
In addition, the float data can help us to look at the biological implications

60
00:03:11,511 --> 00:03:12,570
of ocean processes.

61
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:15,220
Would that help with preserving fish stocks?

62
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:15,270
Yes,

63
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:18,610
and advising governments on fisheries legislation.

64
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:21,880
We're well on the way to completing a project on this.

65
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:26,160
We hope it will help to bring about more sustainable fishing practices.

66
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:28,440
We'll be seeing the results of that quite soon.

67
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:32,670
It sounds like the data from floats has lots of applications.

68
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:34,003
Yes it does.

69
00:03:34,003 --> 00:03:36,670
It's also a powerful agricultural tool.

70
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:41,401
If we were aware of what the weather would be like, say, next year,

71
00:03:41,401 --> 00:03:46,132
we could make sure that the farmers planted appropriate grain varieties to produce the

72
00:03:46,132 --> 00:03:48,160
best yield from the available rainfall.

73
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,248
That sounds a bit like science fiction, especially when now we can't even tell

74
00:03:52,248 --> 00:03:54,000
them when a drought will break.

75
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,674
I agree that this concept is still a long way in the future,

76
00:03:57,674 --> 00:04:01,630
but it will come eventually and the float data will have made a contribution.
